-- title:
--   A transcendental element with finite extension: Mumford field of Γ₊
-- statement:
--   Let $K_0$ be a field and $K$ a $K_0$-algebra field carrying a valuation $v$ with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$, subject to the rank-one condition that for all $x,y \in K$ with $v(x) < 1$ and $y \neq 0$ there is $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $v(x)^n \le v(y)$, and assume $K$ complete and algebraically closed. Let $R_0$ be a discrete valuation domain with a $K_0$-algebra structure making $K_0$ its fraction field, with finite residue field, and such that an element of $K_0$ lies in the image of $R_0$ exactly when its image in $K$ has valuation $\le 1$. Let $\varpi$ be a `PseudoUniformizer` for $K_0 \subset K$, i.e. an element $\varpi.\varpi \in K_0$ whose image in $K$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that every nonzero $a \in K_0$ satisfies $v(\varpi)^N \le v(a) \le v(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N$; let $\varpi_0 \in R_0$ be irreducible with $\varpi_0 \mapsto \varpi.\varpi$, and assume $\varpi$ is exhausting, i.e. every point of `upperHalfPlane K₀ K` lies in one of the affinoids `affinoid ϖ n`. Let $G$ be a group with a homomorphism $\rho : G \to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ and an action on the set of homothety classes of full $R_0$-lattices in $K_0^2$ which preserves adjacency in the Bruhat–Tits tree and satisfies $g \cdot x = \rho(g) \cdot x$ for all $g$, $x$; assume the holomorphic ring `HolRingOf ϖ ρ` is a domain. Let $\Gamma \le G$ be such that every dart of the tree has finite stabiliser in $\rho(\Gamma)$, with finitely many $\Gamma$-orbits of vertices and of darts, and containing an element $\gamma$ that fails to preserve the vertex type relative to the standard vertex. Write $\Gamma_+ = \Gamma \sqcap \mathrm{typePreserving}$ and let $\mathfrak{M}$ be the subfield of $\mathrm{Frac}(\mathrm{HolRingOf}\ \varpi\ \rho)$ of $\Gamma_+$-invariants. Assume further that some subgroup $\Gamma'' \le \Gamma_+$ has nonzero relative index in $\Gamma_+$ and $\rho(\Gamma'')$ torsion-free (every element of finite order is trivial), and that $\mathfrak{M}$ contains an element outside the image of $K$. Then there exists $x \in \mathfrak{M}$ transcendental over $K$ such that $\mathfrak{M}$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$.
--
--   This is the statement that the Mumford field attached to the type-preserving part of $\Gamma$ is the function field of a curve over $K$: it has transcendence degree one and is a finite extension of a rational subfield. It is the analytic input to [`CerednikDrinfeld.Omega.isCurveOver_invariantFieldOf_inf_typePreserving_of_exists_relIndex_ne_zero_of_exists_not_mem_range`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.Omega.isCurveOver_invariantFieldOf_inf_typePreserving_of_exists_relIndex_ne_zero_of_exists_not_mem_range), and is obtained from the description of the valuations of the invariant field together with the criterion [`AlgebraicCurve.transcendental_and_finiteDimensional_adjoin_of_valuations`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.transcendental_and_finiteDimensional_adjoin_of_valuations); the two extra hypotheses (virtual torsion-freeness of the image and existence of a non-constant invariant function) are left as assumptions, to be supplied in the arithmetic instance.
